    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d96">

                <pb facs="0102" n="93" sameAs="frus:frus1940v02/pg93"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">340.1115A/614: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Switzerland</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Harrison</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bern</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-05-16T16:00:00+01:00">May 16, 1940—4
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received May 16—12:44 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>38. My 37, May 16, noon.<anchor n="52" xml:id="d96-f1"/> Consulates now estimate
                    700 potential evacuees under proposed plan. In addition thereto there are others
                    already booked on United States Lines sailings from Genoa on May <pb facs="0103"
                        n="94" xml:id="pg94"/> 18 and June 1. United States Lines, Genoa, reported
                    objecting informally to advice being given to Americans to congregate in
                    Bordeaux area since they are still offering sailings from Genoa.</p>
                <p>Consulates report many Americans especially those with children hesitant to
                    proceed to Bordeaux area unless assurances are given that there will be sailings
                    therefrom regardless of whether hostilities break out in the Mediterranean.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harrison</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
